[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"183","stub":{"id":"183","section":"4) Dos Rios to Alderpoint","altname":"","river":"Eel","difficulty":"IIItoIV","states":["USA-CAL"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"migration-runnable","latestReading":{"value":"1030","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"111365","location":{"latitude":"39.714901","longitude":"-123.350998"},"name":"Put-in","distance":"0","photoID":"l4mKXBr2Qj8SHCpZG09tO","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"Island Mountain is typically the largest and most technical rapid on the run.  While it is straight forward at lower flows, higher flows bring on big waves and hydrolics that can flip boats.\n\nThe rapid can be scouted from the railroad tracks on the left bank above a large cobble bar. At ~5,400 CFS, there are multiple routes through the rapid, most of which have much stronger eddy lines and currents than appears to be the case from high on the bank where people scout the rapid.","id":"08uraTdjXb55wRUYzWcol","location":{"latitude":"40.02354","longitude":"-123.489809"},"name":"Island Mountain","distance":"29.51","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"This rapid is no big deal at lower flows but at 10k it becomes a masive hole and is hard to see from above. The line is on the right and pretty thin.","id":"Rs62xSGaRDDlYkMJoBYjD","location":{"latitude":"40.04145","longitude":"-123.506541"},"name":"Kekawaka Falls","distance":"33.59","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false,"photoID":"0nUyz3gOSG6I3Bb84Y1HY"},{"type":"takeout","description":"Donna can advise which takeout is best accessible depending on the river flow.","id":"116634","location":{"latitude":"40.179811","longitude":"-123.615355"},"name":"Take-out","distance":"48.63","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"883371","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886048","description":"This is the put-in on Outlet Creek, just above the confluence with the Main Eel.  The name comes from the tennis shoes that are hanging off of the power lines marking the turn off to the put-in.","caption":"Tennis Shoe Put-in","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Dave Steindorf","poi_id":"111365","photo_date":"2014-03-14T00:49:07.000Z","updated_at":"2023-02-11T00:49:07.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883371","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883371"}},{"id":"883373","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886050","description":"Very scenic camp.   ","caption":"Spy Rock Camp","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Dave Steindorf","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883373","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883373"}},{"id":"0nUyz3gOSG6I3Bb84Y1HY","ext":"jpg","post_id":"DIyh7ge1iLDkmrqhiv3SJ","description":"At ~5,400 CFS, Kekawaka Falls is a Class IV rapid with 3 distinct parts spread out over half a mile:\n1) a boulder dodge for a couple hundred yards just after the river takes a right turn around a blind corner. This can be scouted on river right. \n2) After the boulder dodge, you'll see a horizon line with the Falls. This can be scouted on the right. It's a Class IV scout with very slippery rocks and some poison oak. The run is down the narrow right tongue, just right of the pour-over rock in the middle of the river.\n3) Another couple hundred yards downstream is a Class III/IV rapid with enormous unavoidable holes, reminiscent of Lower Bernard on Hell's Canyon. ","caption":"Kekawaka Falls (Mile 33.5)","poi_name":null,"subject":"The Falls at Kekawaka Falls rapid","author":"Isaac Swanson","poi_id":"Rs62xSGaRDDlYkMJoBYjD","photo_date":"2024-04-28T04:04:29.000Z","updated_at":"2024-04-28T04:04:29.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/0nUyz3gOSG6I3Bb84Y1HY","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/0nUyz3gOSG6I3Bb84Y1HY"}},{"id":"883374","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886051","description":"Nice Beach. ","caption":"Spy Rock Camp 2","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Dave Steindorf","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883374","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883374"}},{"id":"cRzVpw0V42IVlDMp24Axv","ext":"jpg","post_id":"DIyh7ge1iLDkmrqhiv3SJ","description":"Some of the Eel's rapids can be scouted from the railroad tracks.","caption":"Unnamed Rapid","poi_name":null,"subject":"Unnamed Rapid","author":"Isaac Swanson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-04-28T03:36:02.000Z","updated_at":"2024-04-28T03:36:02.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/cRzVpw0V42IVlDMp24Axv","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/cRzVpw0V42IVlDMp24Axv"}},{"id":"dx8wBoPjuBJXcOEi5inTj","ext":"jpg","post_id":"cWcULkLxFoC1TMoVGbzwx","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Dup Crosson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2021-04-10T19:55:49.000Z","updated_at":"2023-11-30T19:55:49.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/dx8wBoPjuBJXcOEi5inTj","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/dx8wBoPjuBJXcOEi5inTj"}},{"id":"883372","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886049","description":"This is a rail car that is in the middle of the river.  Fortunately, it is in a flat section. You really would not want to swim into this thing. ","caption":"Bad Strainer","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Dave Steindorf","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883372","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883372"}},{"id":"BsSivvmK43UwzrVvqR9gj","ext":"jpg","post_id":"cWcULkLxFoC1TMoVGbzwx","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Dup Crosson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2021-04-10T19:55:49.000Z","updated_at":"2023-11-30T19:55:49.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/BsSivvmK43UwzrVvqR9gj","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/BsSivvmK43UwzrVvqR9gj"}},{"id":"FDRrVFEajIT5uDLkKqCLb","ext":"jpg","post_id":"DIyh7ge1iLDkmrqhiv3SJ","description":"After Mile 34, the river becomes a Class I or II boulder dodge all the way to Alderpoint at ~5,500 CFS, with lots of calm stretches and nice camps.","caption":"Campsite around Mile 34.5","poi_name":null,"subject":"First Campsite after Kekawaka Falls","author":"Isaac Swanson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-04-28T04:04:29.000Z","updated_at":"2024-04-28T04:04:29.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/FDRrVFEajIT5uDLkKqCLb","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/FDRrVFEajIT5uDLkKqCLb"}},{"id":"cIVXjdMQZIOVQGMNWuA8V","ext":"jpg","post_id":"DIyh7ge1iLDkmrqhiv3SJ","description":"Mile 39 Rapid is a Class II read-and-run rapid. It can be scouted from the right bank before the river turns to the right around a blind corner.","caption":"Mile 39 Rapid","poi_name":null,"subject":"Class II rapid at Mile 39","author":"Isaac Swanson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-04-28T04:04:29.000Z","updated_at":"2024-04-28T04:04:29.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/cIVXjdMQZIOVQGMNWuA8V","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/cIVXjdMQZIOVQGMNWuA8V"}},{"id":"l4mKXBr2Qj8SHCpZG09tO","ext":"jpg","post_id":"DIyh7ge1iLDkmrqhiv3SJ","description":"The put in is not signed and is difficult to find if you're not sure exactly what you're looking for. We would not have found it if not for our shuttle driver, Donna, who pointed out that it's on the MAIN STEM of the Eel about half a mile southwest of the bridge into Dos Rios. To reach it, descend an unmarked 4x4 road to a gravel put-in. Note that as of April 2024, the first riffle needed to be lined in order to avoid a tree/log in the main channel and large willows in the secondary channel. Class IV riffle!","caption":"Put-in location as of April 2024","poi_name":null,"subject":"Put in Location as of April 2024","author":"Isaac Swanson","poi_id":"111365","photo_date":"2024-04-28T04:04:29.000Z","updated_at":"2024-04-28T04:04:30.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/l4mKXBr2Qj8SHCpZG09tO","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/l4mKXBr2Qj8SHCpZG09tO"}},{"id":"F3tvlB1ApD0pwXefm1cqq","ext":"jpg","post_id":"DIyh7ge1iLDkmrqhiv3SJ","description":"There is a gorgeous sandy camp around Mile 19. It is the last good campsite before whitewater picks up around the confluence of the North Fork of the Eel.","caption":"Large Sandy Camp ~Mile 19","poi_name":null,"subject":"Large Sandy Camp, Mile 19","author":"Isaac Swanson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-04-28T04:04:29.000Z","updated_at":"2024-04-28T04:04:30.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/F3tvlB1ApD0pwXefm1cqq","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/F3tvlB1ApD0pwXefm1cqq"}}],"detail":{"id":"183","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"This is a great multi-day river trip that is often overlooked but can be worth checking out in the spring. In some years flows last through Memorial Day weekend. Look for something above 1,500 cfs for rafts, but it’s still a good kayak self-support trip at lower flows.\n\nThere is some disagreement about what difficulty level the river is, and at what flows. A previous description of this river stated that 'at moderate flows (1500-10000 cfs) the run is class II with half a dozen class III rapids.' That may be true for expert kayakers. However, as of April 2024, the river is a solid Class IV run at a flow of ~5,400 CFS, and I would consider that flow to be High, with as many as four Class IV rapids and numerous additional Class III+ rapids. The river's remoteness, low visitation, and lack of available mile-by-mile river guides adds to the sense of difficulty. In addition, many of the Class IV rapids appear around blind corners. In general, the difficulty level above 5,000 CFS is somewhere between the Rogue and the Tuolumne.\n\nThis 50-mile trip is typically run over 3 to 5 days with some nice sandy beaches that make for good camping and plenty of opportunities for hiking and exploration up side canyons.\n\nFrom Alderpoint, you can also continue down to Fort Seward (8mi) for more Class II floating, or all the way down to the SF confluence for another day of easy paddling.\n\n**Other Information Sources:**\n\nCassady & Calhoun, Holbek & Stanley, Schwind, Penny\n\n[River Description on California Creekin](http://www.cacreeks.com/eel-main.htm).","averageGradient":"13","maxGradient":null,"length":"48.6","correlations":[{"reachID":"183","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"500","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"10000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"EEL R A FORT SEWARD CA","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"11475000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"1030","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"9.35","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"40.21816919","longitude":"-123.6325017"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/11475000","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/11475000/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"migration-runnable","latestReading":{"value":"1030","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["111365","08uraTdjXb55wRUYzWcol","Rs62xSGaRDDlYkMJoBYjD","116634"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-123.351618,39.714991],[-123.351528,39.715877],[-123.351671,39.718168],[-123.351814,39.718722],[-123.352394,39.719153],[-123.35253,39.719217],[-123.352645,39.719282],[-123.353265,39.719698],[-123.354265,39.720395],[-123.35461,39.72077],[-123.355043,39.721142],[-123.355667,39.721323],[-123.356343,39.721541],[-123.356898,39.721608],[-123.357965,39.721405],[-123.358588,39.721526],[-123.359317,39.721691],[-123.360346,39.721834],[-123.36089,39.721786],[-123.36127,39.721781],[-123.361632,39.721771],[-123.362423,39.722329],[-123.363321,39.722887],[-123.364274,39.723305],[-123.364913,39.723426],[-123.36538,39.723837],[-123.365816,39.723905],[-123.366545,39.724317],[-123.3675,39.724647],[-123.367767,39.725494],[-123.367628,39.726221],[-123.367548,39.726395],[-123.367417,39.726475],[-123.367386,39.7266],[-123.367417,39.726948],[-123.367314,39.727237],[-123.36729,39.727537],[-123.367481,39.728197],[-123.367479,39.728696],[-123.3675,39.72884],[-123.367608,39.729554],[-123.368075,39.73009],[-123.368591,39.730332],[-123.36886,39.730382],[-123.369088,39.730586],[-123.369498,39.73184],[-123.369423,39.732729],[-123.368924,39.733953],[-123.369016,39.734397],[-123.369373,39.735008],[-123.36963,39.735308],[-123.370343,39.735971],[-123.370614,39.736199],[-123.371291,39.736756],[-123.37199,39.737698],[-123.372137,39.738613],[-123.372125,39.739562],[-123.372654,39.740219],[-123.372545,39.741371],[-123.372045,39.742904],[-123.371983,39.743664],[-123.372085,39.744335],[-123.37235,39.744752],[-123.372449,39.744863],[-123.372796,39.745256],[-123.373075,39.745471],[-123.373378,39.745519],[-123.